A||||15||Gold-86SE||Techies I Need Advice - V6 Rear Exhaust Manifold Shield (long, but help is needed)||||1||
Z||000000||Gold-86SE||03-05-2001||11:42 PM||||<BR>OK, I have done the prerequisit search of the archives and really am not satisfied with the results of the returns.  Not that the search was useless, more that the info returned did not help me fully answer my question, so here it goes.<P>I am replacing the exhaust manifold gaskets, and just about everyone knows that there is a shield between the block and exhaust manifold, and I know that the shield was added for recall purposes.  However, I question whether or not it is really needed.<P>I have an 85 Fiero GT with 98k miles and is my project car in my back yard and I can almost swear that it does not have this shield, based on the fact that the air-intake was the original factory issue, not the recall replacement, and that the EGR tube is original. Now, if this vehicle can survive this many miles and not have the shield why would my 86 SE V6 not also survive?<P>My real problem with this shield design is that if actually used, it is shy one exhaust gasket.  It seems to me that there should be a gasket between the block/head and the shield and then another between the shield and exhaust manifold, kinda like a sandwich.<P>To me it should be like this:<BR>block/head<BR>gasket<BR>shield<BR>gasket<BR>e. manifold<P>If the shield is necessary, what is the likely hood that this additional exhaust gasket would make a better seal.  I really don't want to just place the shield against the block without a gasket.  It seems to me that this would lend itself to exhaust leaks.  What do you guys think?<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Cold and Rain Daily Driver)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-06-2001||01:12 AM||||I can only tell you what I have done and what others have done in my club. The shield is thrown away. We have not heard of any problems associtated with it OR without it. Its not like you gain or lose 5 horsepower! Those who do have it do so without additional gaskets and no exhaust leaks.<P>If you want to use it this is what we have found through our experience.<P>Hope this helps!||||reg||4
Z||000002||skitime||03-06-2001||07:45 AM||||I threw mine out too.  It was a stupid idea.  What is it sheilding anyway?<p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 03-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-06-2001||08:29 AM||||i ditched the one on the GT the SE and BlackGTs 86GT. and no ones car has done anything bad...  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||03-06-2001||09:03 AM||||i just replaced my manifolds, and reinstalled the heat shield.  <P>however, i used permatex ultra copper gasket material, and put it on both the shield and manifold, to sandwich it in.  this stuff was recomended by Ed Parks, it is all he uses.<P>i've driven the car about 230-250 miles and there are no leaks.<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000005||Gold-86SE||03-06-2001||09:12 AM||||<BR>Thanks guys for the responses.  I might pull it out, but if I leave it in, it will definately have another gasket.||||reg||1
Z||000006||stsmithgt||03-06-2001||06:34 PM||||The shield is like a crush gasket around the exhaust ports.  I had mine replaced with a kit I ordered from the car dealership.  It came apparently came with all the required new parts for the repair like a new manifolt, nuts, bolts, a big pipe they didn't know what to do with.  I let a large Garage in Sand City CA. install it for me.  It took <B>five</B> visits to get them to do everything correctly (sort of) <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">.... Well that was within two years ago and it's leaking like a mother FFFF again. <BR>Anyway, I didn't like the cheezy looking shield from the get go.  I think I'll throw the damn shield away and get a real gasket for it.  I wouldn't let my car leak bad enough to catch on fire anyway.<BR>I think it's only function was for if you let your valve cover gaskets leaked oil, the shield would channel the oil away from landing directly on the exhaust manifold.  Hence preventing a fire.  One of those <I>FIRE</I> stories again.<P>I found about a 10 page right up on the web about the shield recall.  I'll try to remeber to get the address for it out of my reciept binder.  I ended up spoon feeding their mechanic the information from it so I could get my car back sooner.  The only thing they did well was to waist my time and take my money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||2birds||03-06-2001||09:16 PM||||I just changed my forward exhaust gasket last weekend, and I re-installed both of the heat shields.  I heard about Ed Parks using the ultra copper RTV, but I imagine it would be a real PITA to clean off if I ever had to redo it again.  I went with the Fel-Pro gasket set.<BR>I don't think the flat heat shield would seal very well again, after so many years of corrosion.  I couldn't get a real clean, flat surface anymore on mine.<BR>I cut the top part off of the flat shield, so I could bolt it on the outer side of the manifold flanges, using just the three lower bolts, and put the curved shield back on.  <BR>I used six brand new 8 X 1.25 X 30mm stainless steel bolts, and new stainless lock and flat washers, all courtesy of Lowe's Home Improvement.  I also chased all of the threads with a tap before installing anything.  Didn't break any of the original bolts, either, although I probably used a half can of PB penetrant.<BR>I unbolted the rear of the cradle and lowered it until the dogbone was vertical, and I removed the decklid and battery.  Makes it a whole lot easier, IMHO.  My AC bracket was in the way of the lower right hand manifold bolt, so I finally removed it (AC hasn't worked in 11 years anyway).  The two bolts that hold the bracket also hold the dogbone bracket on, so I needed a shorter bolt for one hole, and a 1.5" spacer for the other one, since it also supports the Oil Pressure sending unit line.<BR>So far, no leaks.  ||||reg||1
Z||000008||skitime||03-06-2001||09:51 PM||||If you study the heat shield between the manifold and head you can see it will not channel oil leaking from the valve cover, because it is under the manifold and not above it. If you ask yourself what is this doing, you have to conclude it is doing very little. There is really nothing under it that it is protecting either. Meanwhile it is doubling the number of places that the manifold can leak. I would recommend replacing all the manifold bolts with the Saturn studs and nuts.  One nice thing about the studs is they have a star head on them so you can turn them in and out. Be sure to use sheet metal nuts to hold the outer sheild so they are easy to romove as well.||||reg||1
Z||000009||2birds||03-06-2001||10:45 PM||||I think the flat shield is there to keep oil from dripping onto the catalytic converter, which runs hotter than the manifold. I don't think moving it to the outside of the manifold flanges hurts.   It's still a PITA to get back in between the block and the coolant tube.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stsmithgt||03-07-2001||09:42 AM||||Sheild keeps oil from dripping on the Cat?  Sounds resonable as any.  I guess they don't think the shield on the cat works so well for those who neglect their car so much that there's enough oil to catch on fire.<BR>That A/C bracket <B>IS</B> a PITA.  Especialy when it has been buggard up from previous mechanics.  Hey FS Terry, you know what mine looks like don't you? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000011||theogre||03-07-2001||08:18 PM||||The sheilds are to prevent a leaking valve cover from dropping oil on the exhuast manifold. On the V6, they may deflect some of the heat away from the valve cover as well.<P>The shields, on the 4 or the 6, are installed without a gasket between it and the block. You could put one there if you really want it.||||reg||1
Z||000012||skitime||03-07-2001||11:05 PM||||The shield is under the manifold too late to deflect oil from the valve covers.  It would have to be on top of the manifold to do any good for that.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Gold-86SE||03-08-2001||09:13 AM||||<BR>Well, I threw out the flat shield that goes between the block and the manifold, but reused the curved, circular shield that bolts to the outside of the manifold.  The circular shield looks like it actually is worth the use, but not the other.||||reg||1
Z||000014||stsmithgt||03-08-2001||09:55 AM||||I've had my center console out fixing it up and boy let me tell ya,  That fire wall sounds paper thin.  I can hear the leak in mine as if it's right next to my ear.  The small leak sounds huge without the console in and it's driving me nuts.  I don't really want to double up on the gaskets to seal that shield in.  Is there a better gasket then just using the one that's used on the other side?  If the shield has justifiable reasons for being their, I was thinking of tweaking it or making one that mounts the same place as the curved one that would do the same job.(hopefully without rattling)....have to find the time to investigate it first. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000015||2birds||03-08-2001||07:57 PM||||I posted about my mod to it earlier here.<P>Basically, I cut the upper portion of the flat shield off so that it can be bolted on with just the lower three manifold bolts, on the outside of the manifold flanges.  No gasket required (except between the flanges and the head, of course), and it still fits in the original location (in relation to the block and head).  It has been almost two weeks, and no rattles or leaks.  I put the curved shield back on, too, but I used stainless bolts in all six holes.  I bolted the manifold and flat shield up first, using the center upper and two outer lower bolts, then I installed the curved shield and the other three bolts.   It's real obvious if you try it this way.||||reg||1
